<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rio Grande</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Río Bravo del Norte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rio Grande is a river that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and flows south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, then forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Its upper valley was the heart of Pueblo country and of Spanish New Mexico. After Texan independence the lands north of its lower course were disputed with Mexico until 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-grande.jpg" alt="Rio Grande" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glysiak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_Grande_in_Big_Bend_NP.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rio Grande, called the Río Bravo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, is 1,896 miles (3,051 km) long. It rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, in the San Juan Mountains, flows through the San Luis Valley and the Rio Grande Gorge into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a> and Las Cruces, and reaches El Paso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and Ciudad Juárez, Chihuahua. South of El Paso it forms the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Mexico as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Its largest tributary by discharge is the Rio Conchos, and the Pecos River drains the largest area. The name means Big River in Spanish; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> call it Tó Baʼáadi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest traces of people in the valley include Folsom sites near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>, possibly from about 10,800 to 9,700 BCE. Drought brought the collapse of the Ancestral Puebloan culture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a> around 1130 CE, and many Ancestral Puebloans migrated to the Rio Grande valley, where most of the Tanoan and Keresan pueblos were founded. In autumn 1540 an expedition under Francisco Vásquez de Coronado reached the Tiwa pueblos along the river, and on 12 July 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> y Salazar founded the Spanish colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> beside the Ohkay Owingeh Pueblo.</p><p>In the late 1830s and early 1840s the river marked the disputed border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, which Mexico placed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueces-river">Nueces River</a>. The disagreement was part of the reason for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1846, and since 1848 the river has marked the boundary from El Paso and Ciudad Juárez to the Gulf. In the nineteenth century over 200 steamboats worked between its mouth and Rio Grande City, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> the Mexican port of Bagdad at its mouth was the Confederacy's only legitimate port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is a vital source of water for seven American and Mexican states and flows mostly through arid and semi-arid land. Its water is shared under treaties signed in 1906 and 1944 and under the Rio Grande Compact of 1938 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. Dams include Elephant Butte, Amistad and Falcon. A stretch of 260 miles in New Mexico and Texas is designated the Rio Grande Wild and Scenic River. The main border crossings include Ciudad Juárez and El Paso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a> and Nuevo Laredo, and Brownsville and Matamoros.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rio Grande” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century, raiders crossed the <b>Rio Grande</b>, wintered in the Bolsón de Mapimí and struck as far south as Durango, Zacatecas and San Luis Potosí.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic's claims outran its reach: it took the <b>Rio Grande</b> as its boundary, including eastern New Mexico, which Mexico administered throughout, and hoped for a railroad to the Gulf of California; much of the disputed land was Comanche country outside either state's control, and some districts sent…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ancestral Puebloans are the ancestors of the Pueblo peoples of Arizona and New Mexico: the Hopi, the Zuni, the people of Acoma and Laguna and the pueblos of the <b>Rio Grande</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>On the <b>Rio Grande</b>, Silvia Hector Webber and her husband John Ferdinand Webber ferried people into Mexico, and most of the help in the south came from Mexican labourers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress approved President Polk's request for war after clashes near the <b>Rio Grande</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Zachary Taylor's artillery won the first major battle of the Mexican-American War near the <b>Rio Grande</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-8">8 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
